= Unpose =

#type: node
#context: sop
#internal: a3ob_unpose
#tags: arma, p3d

"""Moves your weighted mesh from the pose of the fitted skeleton back onto the Arma rest pose, by a cage from your body to the sample body."""

Weight Transfer and Weight Cleanup weight your mesh in the pose of the fitted skeleton, the one Rig Fit made inside your mesh. Arma needs the mesh in its own rest pose. This node moves every point back: it applies the inverse of the bone matrices that carry the Arma skeleton to the fitted one, blended with the weights of the point. The weights are not changed.

Connect your weighted mesh to input 1, the output of Rig Fit as it is to input 2 (its fitted skeleton is used) and the Arma skeleton to input 3. The move of each bone is worked out from the joint positions only: how the bone turned, and how much longer or shorter it is along the bone (a longer arm is not a thicker arm). A perfect fit moves nothing. The message gives the mean and the largest move of the points, and the bones whose points moved most. The Arma rest pose has the pelvis at the origin, so these moves include the walk from where your character stands to there, about a metre.

A rigid piece (a helmet or a pouch made by Accessory Weights in Rigid mode) is moved by one matrix for all its points, so it keeps its shape. The unpose can only remove what the fitted skeleton describes: a landmark that is a few centimetres off in Rig Fit leaves the mesh a few centimetres off here.

@inputs

Your mesh:
    Your mesh with its cleaned bone weights, for example from Weight Cleanup.

Fitted skeleton:
    The output of Rig Fit as it is (its fitted skeleton, fitted body and rest body are used).

Arma skeleton:
    The Arma skeleton, for example from Rig Skeleton. It must have the same bones as the fitted one.

@parameters

Method:
    #id: method

    How the points move back to the Arma rest pose. Neither method changes the overall size.

    Type: Menu. Default: Cage.

    Cage:
        Move the points by a cage: the sample body wrapped onto your body (fitted body of Rig Fit) is taken to the sample body in the Arma rest pose (Point Deform). It follows your body's proportions. Rigid pieces then move by one rigid transform each, and loose clothes and gear (points outside the Body Group) are carried by the wrapped body's surface at their own distance.

    Bones:
        Move each point by the inverse of its blended bone matrix, from the fitted skeleton to the Arma one. Fast, but it cannot express that your body has other proportions than the sample (thighs can telescope, hands crumple).

Body Group:
    #id: bodygroup

    Cage method: the point group of your mesh that is the bare body, the one Rig Fit wrapped onto. Its points always follow the cage. A piece outside it whose points follow one bone only (a helmet) is moved as a rigid piece; other points outside it (clothes that bend) keep their distance from the closest point of the wrapped body and move with that place of its surface. Empty means the whole mesh is body.

    Type: Group picker (text; the menu lists the point groups of the input, the arrow button picks in the viewport). Default: body.

Rigid Pieces Keep Their Shape:
    #id: rigid_pieces

    Move all the points of a rigid piece (made by Accessory Weights in Rigid mode, or an island outside the Body Group that follows one bone only) with one rigid transform, so the piece keeps its shape. Off: every point follows the cage (or its own weights in the Bones method), which can stretch a rigid piece.

    Type: On or off. Default: On.

Group:
    #id: group

    The points to unpose. Empty means all points. The menu lists the point groups of the input and shows the P3D name of a selection; picking adds or removes the group. The arrow button picks points in the viewport. You can also type group names, patterns or point numbers (for example 0-3).

    Type: Group picker (text; the menu lists the point groups of the input, the arrow button picks in the viewport). Default: (empty).

The full page for this node is docs/user/reference/a3ob_unpose.html in the A3OB folder. Open it in a web browser.
